{"name":"Hibiscus Petal","hex":"#edaaac","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hibiscus","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flowering plant of the genus Hibiscus, especially Hibiscus syriacus, found in tropical to temperate regions, of some species used for making infusions/tea.","A cocktail made with champagne and cranberry juice."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hibiscus"},{"term":"petal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the component parts of the corolla of a flower. It applies particularly, but not necessarily only, when the corolla consists of separate parts, that is when the petals are not connately fused. Petals are often brightly colored.","The labia (majora)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To spread out from, like the petals of a flower"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/petal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hibiscus","blocks":[{"text":"Hibiscus is a genus of plants in the family Malvaceae native to the tropics and subtropics. The genus is quite large, comprising 319 species. Hibiscus are renowned for their large, showy flowers and those species are commonly known simply as \"hibiscus\", or less widely known as rose mallow. The genus includes both annual and perennial herbaceous plants, as well as woody shrubs and small trees."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Hibiscus_flower_TZ.jpg/330px-Hibiscus_flower_TZ.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hibiscus","wikidata":"Q157124"},{"title":"Petal","blocks":[{"text":"Petals are modified leaves that form an inner whorl surrounding the reproductive parts of flowers. They are often brightly coloured or unusually shaped to attract pollinators. All of the petals of a flower are collectively known as the corolla."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Mature_flower_diagram.svg/330px-Mature_flower_diagram.